     1	# The Orchestration Process
     2	
     3	How this project is actually built: a human researcher directing a
     4	multi-model AI system whose workflow is itself a deliberate, versioned,
     5	self-instrumenting piece of engineering. This document is the single
     6	in-repo description of that process. (The executable playbooks live
     7	outside the repository as reusable "skills" so they transfer to future
     8	projects; this page describes what they do and where their evidence
     9	lands in this repo.) Binding role and process changes live in
    10	`docs/decision_log.md`; this page avoids copying volatile model versions.
    11	
    12	## Roles: a lead, independent implementers/reviewers, and a human at the top
    13	
    14	- **Ed (researcher)** sets research direction, methodology
    15	  non-negotiables (raw-evidence bundles, dual-basis capture with gross-energy
    16	  headlines, named
    17	  measurement boundaries, no unauditable claims), hardware/access
    18	  decisions, and — critically — *process policy*: every rule below
    19	  traces to a standing instruction issued after an observed failure or
    20	  opportunity. External-facing claims and merge authority derive from
    21	  him (he granted the lead conditional self-merge authority on
    22	  2026-07-08 once the review gate had proven itself).
    23	- **The designated lead** owns
    24	  decomposition, triage, design adjudication, every final diff gate,
    25	  all live/hardware verification, merge decisions, bookkeeping, and
    26	  process evolution. Other agents save lead capacity without inheriting
    27	  final authority; all escalation paths terminate at the lead.
    28	- **Independent implementation and review agents** do the heavy reading and
    29	  writing: implementation against pinned specs, adversarial review
    30	  lenses, test writing, test *auditing* (never of its own tests — a
    31	  fresh instance audits), docs drafting, and review of the lead's own
    32	  consequential decisions. Cross-model review is load-bearing by
    33	  design: the attributed per-layer catch record (below) shows the two
    34	  roles consistently catching different classes of defect.
    35	- **Specialist agents** handle bounded sweeps (for example, docs
    36	  consistency) and, when a stream genuinely needs
    37	  mid-stream judgment, as a stream director. AMENDED by D-129
    38	  (Ed, 2026-08-09): lieutenant-directed executor lanes are the standing
    39	  default shape under the lead-token economy (current model assignments
    40	  live in D-129 and the memory index, not here) — the "exception rather
    41	  than the default" framing below reflects the C-009/C-010 record of
    42	  its day and is superseded for current operation (see the decision log).
    43	- **Image-heavy analysis uses the designated image-capable review route** per
    44	  C-012, after the site-observatory stream's image-critique rounds.
    45	- **Invited-peer validation is allowed to overturn lead designs**; C-014
    46	  recorded two lead designs overturned by an invited peer before
    47	  implementation.
    48	
    49	## The loop, end to end
    50	
    51	Every substantial session runs one conductor procedure:
    52	
    53	1. **Intake** — read `RUN_STATE.md` (the intake pointer), the task
    54	   queue, the latest run report; never re-decide anything the decision
    55	   log settled.
    56	2. **Decompose** — split work into genuinely independent streams
    57	   (disjoint expected diff footprints), one git worktree + branch each;
    58	   assign each stream a review tier by *cost of being wrong*
    59	   (measurement-semantics and contract-bearing work gets the full
    60	   pipeline; docs get a light tier). Preflight gates: hardware-shaped
    61	   streams require a confirmed device inventory; anything pinned
    62	   without live validation carries a PROVISIONAL label; measurement
    63	   sessions require a no-agent "quiet machine" lock.
    64	3. **Per-stream pipeline** — for each reviewable unit: an invited
    65	   design-argument round (the implementer must argue trade-offs before
    66	   coding), implementation, then a layered review stack:
    67	   2–3 fresh-instance counterreview lenses over the diff → lead triage
    68	   with recorded dispositions → fixes → a dedicated test-amplification
    69	   round (an independent writer adds edge-case tests) → a
    70	   writer≠reviewer test audit (a fresh instance hunts tautological,
    71	   vacuous, or wrong-expectation tests) → the lead's diff gate.
    72	4. **Lead live gates** — never delegated: the lead runs the real flow
    73	   (real corpus, real CLI, real hardware where present). This layer has
    74	   repeatedly caught blockers no other layer saw, including defects
    75	   whose own tests were green because the tests encoded the same wrong
    76	   assumption as the code.
    77	5. **Merge gate** — multi-commit series land as branch + PR. Before any
    78	   merge: a pre-merge oversight pass by 2–3 fresh reviewers with
    79	   distinct angles (deep regression hunt; claim-to-evidence trace;
    80	   merge-order simulation across sibling PRs), lead triage, fixes, CI
    81	   green. **Final-head rule:** any commit that lands after the last
    82	   review round gets one more fresh review before merge — no commit
    83	   merges unreviewed, however small (its first application caught a
    84	   crash path in a "trivial" post-review fix).
    85	6. **Integration review** — after parallel streams merge, one dedicated
    86	   review hunts *interaction* defects no single-stream review can see.
    87	   Its catches are definitionally unique (first outing: two).
    88	7. **Bookkeeping** — a single session record (run report) with a
    89	   verbatim process-trace appendix; the intake pointer and queue
    90	   refreshed; a delegated docs-consistency sweep before the final
    91	   commit (its latest pass found 15 real drift items; earlier passes
    92	   found 5–6). Large documentation batches add the pre-commit
    93	   docs-verify mode; the `consistency-sweep` skill owns that shape,
    94	   including the D-043 supersession check.
    95	8. **Same-session distillation** — lessons fold into the process
    96	   playbooks the same session they are learned. Measured effect: one
    97	   failure mode recurred five times before its fix was distilled, zero
    98	   times after. The current operation-loop also runs its §0
    99	   primary-deliverable check and §8 shipped-check before the session is
   100	   considered done.
   101	9. **Post-landing verification and close-out** — landed work gets the
   102	   matching verification workflow with severity-tiered refuters. Sessions
   103	   that change front-facing state refresh `docs/site/DRIFT.md`; no agent
   104	   regenerates or deploys the site. Automation informs and Ed deploys
   105	   manually, per D-068 and `RUN_STATE.md` end-of-work step 8.
   106	10. **Meta-review (the final step)** — event-driven, not calendar-driven:
   107	    when a review layer stops earning its keep, when an intervention
   108	    repeats despite a folded fix, or when the user asks, the loop is
   109	    reviewed with its own evidence discipline (see Topology for the
   110	    consensus one such review produced). After large workloads the
   111	    post-large-workload meta-reassessment (owned by operation-loop §10)
   112	    always fires, and it runs LAST.
   113	
   114	### Stop cards and paused work
   115	
   116	When a session stops with live work in progress, the lead creates or
   117	updates an `ACTIVE_STOP_CARD` at the top of `RUN_STATE.md`. While active,
   118	that card is the single restart authority and overrides every lower
   119	"what next" list, queue rank, mission guide, and run-report default.
   120	
   121	A stop card must name:
   122	
   123	- the resume authority and exact artifact pointer,
   124	- the reason for stopping,
   125	- worktrees, branches, PRs, and off-repo artifacts that must not be
   126	  cleaned accidentally,
   127	- status terms for each paused item,
   128	- the first resume action, and
   129	- the clearance criteria.
   130	
   131	Use these status terms for paused work:
   132	
   133	| Term | Meaning |
   134	|---|---|
   135	| `APPLIED_UNVERIFIED` | A worker reports code or docs are applied, but the lead has not gated the diff. Not merge-safe. |
   136	| `LEAD_GATED` | The lead has reviewed and run the required local/live checks for the item. |
   137	| `PR_OPEN_CI_GREEN` | A PR exists and CI is green, but merge authority has not yet fired. |
   138	| `MERGED` | The accepted work has landed on main. |
   139	| `UNREAD_UNADJUDICATED` | A report/synthesis exists but has not been consumed into decisions, queue rows, or rejected findings. |
   140	| `ADJUDICATED` | Findings have explicit accept/reject/defer disposition and downstream artifacts are updated. |
   141	
   142	Before an intentional pause, do the minimal stop sync even if full
   143	bookkeeping cannot fit: update only `RUN_STATE.md`'s stop card and the
   144	rank-0 queue row. That is enough to prevent accidental bypass.
   145	
   146	## The artifact system (where rigor becomes auditable)
   147	
   148	Each fact has exactly one home; everything else points at it:
   149	
   150	| Artifact | Role |
   151	|---|---|
   152	| `docs/decision_log.md` | Binding design decisions, each with alternatives considered, consequences, and revisit conditions. The log is the count authority; nothing re-decides these silently. |
   153	| `docs/council_log.md` | The deliberation record: review-council positions, reasoning exchanged, who prevailed, overridden dissents — so a future reader can reconstruct *why*, not just *what*. The log is the range/count authority. |
   154	| `docs/contracts/` | Claim/evidence contracts: `claims_ladder.md` (D-037) plus `analysis_plans.md` (D-038) form the claim gate; strict validation is the evidence ticket. |
   155	| `docs/stream_logs/` | Per-stream decision ledgers, committed WITH the code they justify: every non-trivial in-stream decision (`A-1..A-30`, `B-1..B-46`, …) with mandatory evidence pointers; wrong pins are SUPERSEDED in place, never erased. |
   156	| `docs/run_reports/` | One record per working session: outcomes, verification evidence, a per-layer catch/yield table, the delegation-calibration ledger, restart instructions. |
   157	| `docs/process/state_kernel.json` | Source of truth for work selection: active gates, dependencies, and machine-state lanes ([QUIET-MAC] / [AGENT] / [ED-EXTERNAL]). |
   158	| `TASK_QUEUE.md` | Generated detailed queue projection plus dated history; do not hand-copy its live rows into reader docs. |
   159	| `RUN_STATE.md` | Intake pointer with the generated restart projection. History lives in run reports. |
   160	| `docs/risk_register.md` | Live risks with triggers and mitigation states. |
   161	
   162	Instrumentation ledgers close the loop on the process itself:
   163	
   164	- **Per-layer yield:** every review layer's unique catches are
   165	  attributed and tallied per session under D-061 (C-027; replaces the
   166	  earlier two-zero-sessions auto-drop, which the integration-review
   167	  zero/zero/five sequence falsified): applicability is decided by
   168	  PRE-DECLARED mechanical predicates; outcomes are classified
   169	  accepted-unique-defect / duplicate / clean-verification /
   170	  false-positive-suppression (suppression is not a catch); severity
   171	  weights are fixed before the session; three applicable exposures
   172	  TRIGGER an expected-loss review decision, never automatic deletion;
   173	  safety/final-head/integration layers are never auto-dropped on
   174	  zero-defect streaks. (One layer, the default specialist review lens, was
   175	  dropped under the old rule before D-061.)
   176	- **Delegation calibration:** every delegated unit gets a row — task
   177	  altitude (pinned-spec / design-freedom / judgment-call), outcome
   178	  (assigned by the lead after the gate, never self-labeled), catches,
   179	  and lead rework minutes, with prompt-defects separated from
   180	  model-defects. Delegation boundaries move on this evidence, not
   181	  vibes. Current signal: pinned-spec delegation runs essentially
   182	  defect-free; the serious defects cluster in volunteered additions and
   183	  design-freedom wire contracts — which is exactly where the full lens
   184	- **Invocation manifest:** substantial delegated/tool/skill runs get a
   185	  lightweight manifest row per invocation. Minimum fields:
   186	  `run_id`, `parent_report`, `role_or_lens`, `model`, `wrapper`,
   187	  `session_id`, `prompt_sha256`, `prompt_path`, `output_path`, `status`,
   188	  `consumed_by`, `disposition`, and `commit_or_pr`. Raw logs can stay
   189	  out of git; every ephemeral artifact still needs a committed pointer
   190	  row with `path`, `sha256` or stable id, `promoted_to`, and
   191	  `not_promoted_reason`.
   192	
   193	## Council discipline
   194	
   195	Councils are expensive instruments. Use a full council for methodology,
   196	measurement validity, schema/contract changes, claim boundaries, hardware
   197	protocols, or explicit user requests. For ordinary implementation, use a
   198	small number of targeted lenses plus lead adjudication.
   199	
   200	Every high-impact council must leave a durable scorecard:
   201	
   202	- unique catches by severity,
   203	- accepted/rejected/deferred/false-positive counts,
   204	- lead triage and rework time when practical,
   205	- shipped artifacts,
   206	- queue rows created or re-ranked,
   207	- decision-log IDs promoted, and
   208	- a disposition table: finding → ruling → owner → artifact/queue/decision
   209	  target → closure check.
   210	
   211	Deferred decision-log promotion is itself a tracked obligation, not
   212	ambient prose in a report.
   213	
   214	## Spend guardrails (WO-022; R2 ruled, Ed-ratified 2026-07-13)
   215	
   216	The following policy text is the R2-ratified section, landed verbatim per
   217	audit work order WO-022 (`docs/reviews/2026-07-13-comprehensive-audit/`).
   218	
   219	SPEND GUARDRAILS (capstone benchmark bands) — provisional calibration constants; review after two completed arcs; sunset at capstone submission.
   220	
   221	1. ACCOUNTING SOURCE. Sol spend: `codex-usage` local accounting (the standing snapshot convention), corroborated by codex-run-v3 manifest `token_usage` rows where populated. The extraction window must cover the full arc — sum incremental snapshots for multi-day arcs; a single trailing-24h view is insufficient. Fable spend: estimated from local usage accounting; each snapshot names its method and price-table version. Price table v2026-07 (pinned until amended): GPT-5.6-sol $5/$30 per M in/out, cached input $0.50; Fable 5 $10/$50, cache reads $1. All figures are estimates, not billing truth, and are recorded as such. Missing data is recorded as `accounting_unknown`, never as zero.
   222	
   223	2. DENOMINATOR AND CACHED-TOKEN TREATMENT. Token bands count total tokens (cached + uncached, all directions) exactly as codex-usage reports them — cached tokens are never excluded (exclusion invites cache-heavy gaming). Dollar figures apply cached pricing honestly. Cross-family aggregate ceilings bind in combined estimated dollars, because raw cross-family token sums are not commensurable (C-028: Sol ~180x the token volume, Fable ~3.4x the cost).
   224	
   225	3. BOUNDARIES AND ATTRIBUTION. An arc = one council-log C-row, opened at its first delegated session, closed at its closeout snapshot. A work order = one WO/task id. Failed calls, retries, resumes, refuters, fix rounds, delta re-audits, lead usage, and subagents all count against the initiating WO and arc. Arcs and WOs may not be split, renamed, or reopened to reset counters.
   226	
   227	4. BANDS. Each dimension is independent. SOFT crossing = record-and-continue: flag in the spend snapshot plus a one-line justification in the council row. HARD crossing = pause-and-ask Ed before any NEW delegated work in that category; in-flight sessions finish; quiet-machine measurement is never interrupted.
   228	
   229	   | Scope | Soft | Hard |
   230	   |---|---|---|
   231	   | Sol high session | 6M tokens | 12M |
   232	   | Sol xhigh session | 8M | 16M |
   233	   | Sol ultra session | 40M | 60M |
   234	   | Bench-effort WO | 10M / 3 Sol sessions / ~$40 combined | 20M / 6 / ~$80 |
   235	   | Session-effort WO | 30M / 8 Sol sessions / ~$100 combined | 60M / 12 / ~$200 |
   236	   | Arc | 100M / 25 Sol sessions / ~$400 combined / 6 Sol active-hours / 2 elapsed days | 200M / 40 / ~$800 / 12 h / 4 days |
   237	
   238	   WO dollar figures are best-effort: when per-WO Fable attribution is accounting_unknown, the token/session pair binds. Ultra: at most 2 INTENDED ultra sessions per arc, each with a pre-run recorded statement of why xhigh is insufficient and what bounded subagent work it will perform; an unintended ultra is recorded as an anomaly and still counts.
   239	
   240	   Calibration anchors (recorded so recalibration stays honest): healthy xhigh ≈ 2.3–3.5M tokens/session (C-030 post effort-fix; C-028 average); the recorded broken state averaged ~9M. C-028 (330.6M / 59 sessions / ~$1,050 / ~17.5h) crosses every substantive arc HARD dimension — it is the anti-example. The 2026-07-13 comprehensive audit (~30 Sol sessions + ~70 Fable agents, Ed-authorized) crosses arc SOFT on session count only — the intended "exceptional: justify and continue" outcome.
   241	
   242	5. CHECKPOINTS (procedural; owner = the Fable lead). (a) At arc open: predeclare one accepted deliverable increment for the arc — a corpus/measurement result, analysis/figure/report increment, evaluator requirement, or cited advancement of a D-060 gate — and classify planned delegated work as deliverable-facing or process-facing (mixed sessions count as process-facing unless separately attributable). (b) Before each next delegated call: check the completed session against its tier band (a lightweight glance, not a full snapshot); no runtime killing is promised — evaluation happens on completed sessions before any resume, replacement, or new call. (c) At WO close and arc close: take the spend snapshot and evaluate all bands. One missed checkpoint blocks new process-facing delegation until reconciled.
   243	
   244	6. DELIVERABLE-PROGRESS TRIPWIRE (binds while ANY D-060 gate is unmet). If process-facing combined estimated cost exceeds 33% of arc cost OR $250 — whichever occurs first — HARD pause-and-ask Ed before further process-facing delegation. Independently, an arc that closes with process-facing spend but NO accepted deliverable increment pauses further non-exempt process work even if the 33% threshold was not crossed.
   245	
   246	7. EXCEPTIONS AND OVERRIDES. Gate-closing work is deliverable-facing by definition. Correctness-defect and data-preservation work may override the allocation tripwire but is always fully counted. Ed direction is NOT a blanket exemption (the finding's own exemplar — bridge v1/v1.1 — was Ed-directed): it is a recorded override carrying an incremental budget and an expiry, and its spend counts.
   247	
   248	8. NAMED-FAILURE BAR FOR PROCESS INNOVATION. Any NEW process layer, tool, skill, wrapper version, or contract version requires a decision-log entry citing: (a) a named recorded failure it addresses, (b) why existing controls did not cover it, (c) the smallest new layer that does, (d) its applicability predicate, (e) its marginal budget, (f) a sunset/retirement condition. Absent that entry, it is D-060 breadth and waits for the gates.
   249	
   250	9. KEEP-DEFENDER GUARANTEE. No cap consequence auto-deletes a review layer. Layers with recorded unique catches survive any cap; a cap response batches, rescopes, or seeks Ed approval — never silently drops catch-bearing gates (final-head, integration, lead-live, claim/evidence safeguards).
   251	
   252	10. MECHANISM. This policy is PROCEDURAL, not mechanical: no checker script is built now (adding process code during an overengineering correction is self-defeating; the arithmetic is a handful of numbers already recorded at existing snapshot points). Two recorded accounting discrepancies or skipped checkpoints across two arcs constitute the named failure that authorizes an executable checker with tests — via the §8 bar.
   253	
   254	11. RECALIBRATION AND OWNERSHIP. Band values are calibration constants: the lead may amend SOFT values at arc close with evidence via a one-line decision-log amendment; HARD values and this section's structure are Ed-owned. Scheduled review after two completed arcs. The whole policy sunsets at capstone submission.
   255	
   256	Court amendments (T09, folded at the register): canonical session
   257	inclusion uses manifest-corroborated sessions with known token records;
   258	resume rows are deduplicated; non-manifest sessions are reported only as
   259	a labeled upper bound. The 2026-07-13 audit anchor in section 4 is
   260	estimated/accounting_unknown until an audit-close spend snapshot receipts
   261	it (docs/reviews/2026-07-13-comprehensive-audit/receipts/
   262	WO-022-audit-close-spend.json); only a receipted anchor may be used for
   263	recalibration.
   264	
   265	## Topology: how it evolved (an example of the loop improving itself)
   266	
   267	- **v1 (2026-07-07 AM):** per-stream Fable orchestrator subagents, each
   268	  driving its own Codex thread. Worked, but expensive at the apex tier.
   269	- **v2 (2026-07-07 PM):** Opus orchestrators directing Codex, Fable
   270	  apex-only. Ran four streams — and surfaced a structural flaw: subagent
   271	  orchestrators are not woken by their childrens' completion (the "wake
   272	  gap"), forcing the lead to babysit with heartbeats. The session's own
   273	  trace captured two fleet-wide stalls.
   274	- **Meta-review (C-009):** a signed cross-model consensus — two blind
   275	  Codex analyses vs. the lead's blind position, one conferral round —
   276	  produced a hybrid: the lead drives pipeline-shaped streams directly
   277	  (inheriting the harness's only reliable wake guarantee); subagent
   278	  directors are reserved for judgment-heavy streams.
   279	- **Validation (C-010, 2026-07-08):** the first full session under the
   280	  new topology ran ~26 Codex sessions across four streams — resume

