   267	
   268	Considerations: the deciding factors are measurement integrity (controller
   269	does nothing during the measured window) and auditability (the raw sampler
   270	output lands in `raw/` untouched, and `power_trace.csv` is derived from it,
   271	so a parsing bug can be fixed and re-reduced without re-running hardware).
   272	This also keeps the v1 controller single-threaded and therefore simple to
   273	reason about and test.
   274	
   275	Consequences: every real telemetry adapter defines: spawn command, raw output
   276	path under `raw/`, stop mechanism, and a parser raw -> `PowerSample` rows.
   277	The controller never reads samples mid-run; live progress display is out of
   278	scope for v1.
   279	
   280	Revisit when: a backend appears that cannot run as a file-writing subprocess,
   281	or sub-second live feedback becomes a requirement.
   282	
   283	---
   284	
   285	## D-003: Timestamp and clock-alignment policy
   286	
   287	- Date: 2026-06-09
   288	- Status: accepted
   289	- Phase: 2 (single node), 3 (multi node)
   290	
   291	Context: every event and power sample carries `timestamp_s`. Reducers join
   292	events to traces by time. Phase 3 joins traces across two machines, so clock
   293	error becomes energy-attribution error.
   294	
   295	Options considered:
   296	
   297	1. Monotonic clock only. Pro: immune to wall-clock steps. Con: meaningless
   298	   across processes and machines; cannot align controller, sampler subprocess,
   299	   and remote nodes.
   300	2. Wall clock (epoch UTC) only. Pro: universal meaning. Con: NTP steps/slew
   301	   can distort intervals mid-run.
   302	3. Epoch UTC (`time.time()`) as the canonical `timestamp_s` everywhere, with
   303	   per-process monotonic-vs-wall offset recorded in metadata, NTP sync state
   304	   recorded per node, and controller-mediated marker events bounding
   305	   cross-node offset for split runs.
   306	
   307	Decision: option 3.
   308	
   309	Considerations: at our sampling rates (~1-10 Hz) the precision we need is
   310	tens of milliseconds; LAN NTP holds well under that. The marker procedure
   311	(controller timestamps a no-op command on the remote node immediately before
   312	and after each remote stage; round-trip halving bounds the offset) gives a
   313	recorded, per-run error bound rather than an assumption. Monotonic-only would
   314	make multi-process correlation impossible, and our samplers are separate
   315	processes by D-002.
   316	
   317	Consequences: `metadata.json` gains `clock` fields (ntp_synced, estimated
   318	offset bound, method). The methodology doc gets a Clock Synchronization
   319	section (done in the same change as this entry). Reducers must treat
   320	cross-node intervals shorter than the recorded offset bound as unreliable and
   321	flag them in measurement quality.
   322	
   323	Revisit when: sampling moves to >=100 Hz (wall meters with fast export) or a
   324	target cannot run NTP.
   325	
   326	---
   327	
   328	## D-004: `powermetrics` privilege workflow
   329	
   330	- Date: 2026-06-09
   331	- Status: accepted
   332	- Phase: 2
   333	
   334	Context: Phase 1 evidence shows `/usr/bin/powermetrics` exists and "must be
   335	invoked as the superuser". Automated benchmark runs need a non-interactive,
   336	auditable way to obtain that privilege. Hard rule inherited from Phase 1:
   337	document the privilege workflow, never bypass it in code.
   338	
   339	Options considered:
   340	
   341	1. Run the whole controller as root. Pro: simple. Con: massively
   342	   over-privileged; everything the harness writes becomes root-owned; worst
   343	   auditability.
   344	2. Interactive `sudo` prompt at run start. Pro: zero configuration. Con:
   345	   breaks unattended/repeated runs (sudo timeout mid-experiment), and an
   346	   agent cannot answer the prompt.
   347	3. A `sudoers` rule scoped to exactly `/usr/bin/powermetrics` (NOPASSWD) for
   348	   the benchmark user, installed once by the user, documented in the
   349	   Phase 1 exit checklist's instrumentation section. Controller pre-checks
   350	   capability with
   351	   `sudo -n /usr/bin/powermetrics -n 1 -i 100` style probe and fails with
   352	   structured `permission_denied` if absent.
   353	4. A setuid wrapper binary. Con: writing setuid programs to avoid a sudoers
   354	   line is strictly worse on every axis.
   355	
   356	Decision: option 3, with option 2 documented as the manual fallback for
   357	one-off runs.
   358	
   359	Considerations: the scoped sudoers rule grants exactly one binary, owned by
   360	Apple, that only reads telemetry. The pre-check converts a mid-run privilege
   361	failure into an up-front structured failure, which the Phase 1 contract
   362	(structured outcomes, not crashes) requires. Passwords never appear in code,
   363	configs, or logs.
   364	
   365	Consequences: the powermetrics adapter (Slice 2H) always invokes via
   366	`sudo -n`; the Phase 1 exit checklist's instrumentation section gains the
   367	exact sudoers line for the user to install during the 2026-06-10 auth
   368	session; `permission_denied` failures tell the operator precisely what to
   369	add.
   370	
   371	Revisit when: macOS changes powermetrics privilege requirements, or the
   372	measurement machine is shared and the owner declines the sudoers rule (then
   373	runs become operator-attended, option 2).
   374	
   375	---
