   578	## D-010: Run ID scheme
   579	
   580	**2026-08-07 supersession note:** The clause later superseded by D-022 is
   581	retained unchanged as historical context. Current rule ownership: D-022.
   582	
   583	- Date: 2026-06-09
   584	- Status: accepted
   585	- Phase: 2+
   586	
   587	Context: bundle directories need unique, sortable, informative names. The
   588	config has an optional `run_id` field.
   589	
   590	Options considered:
   591	
   592	1. UUID4. Pro: unique. Con: opaque; directory listings become unreadable;
   593	   sorting is meaningless.
   594	2. Monotonic counter. Con: requires global state; collides across machines.
   595	3. `<UTC timestamp>__<target_id>__<workload_name>__<4 hex chars>`, e.g.
   596	   `20260610T142233Z__macbook_m3_max__smoke_short__a1b2`. Components
   597	   sanitized to `[a-z0-9_-]`. If the config supplies `run_id`, it is used
   598	   verbatim after sanitization, with a collision check that fails the run
   599	   rather than overwriting (bundles are immutable evidence). Repetition
   600	   members append `__r<N>` (D-005).
   601	
   602	Decision: option 3.
   603	
   604	Considerations: timestamp prefix makes `ls runs/` chronological; embedded
   605	target/workload makes manual triage possible without opening files; the hex
   606	suffix prevents same-second collisions; refusing to overwrite enforces the
   607	"bundles are evidence" rule mechanically.
   608	
   609	Consequences: bundle writer owns ID generation and sanitization; tests cover
   610	collision behavior.
   611	
   612	Revisit when: never expected.
   613	
