   675	
   676	## D-012: Failure-reason to run-status mapping
   677	
   678	- Date: 2026-06-09
   679	- Status: accepted
   680	- Phase: 2+
   681	
   682	Context: `RunStatus` has `succeeded | failed | unsupported` and
   683	`FailureReason` has eight codes, but nothing defined which reasons produce
   684	which status. The distinction matters: `unsupported` is a *finding* the
   685	capstone reports (hardware applicability results); `failed` is a *defect or
   686	environment problem* to fix.
   687	
   688	Options considered:
   689	
   690	1. Let each adapter pick the status. Con: inconsistent semantics across
   691	   backends; the same condition becomes a finding on one target and a bug on
   692	   another.
   693	2. A fixed mapping table owned by the controller:
   694	   - `unsupported`: `did_not_fit`, `format_unavailable`,
   695	     `unsupported_workload`, `runtime_unavailable`, `telemetry_unavailable`
   696	     (structural incompatibility of the hardware/runtime/model/workload
   697	     combination).
   698	   - `failed`: `permission_denied`, `transport_unavailable`, `unknown_error`
   699	     (operational problems that a configuration or environment change should
   700	     fix).
   701	
   702	Decision: option 2.
   703	
   704	Considerations: the dividing principle is "would we publish this outcome as a
   705	result?" A model that does not fit in 8 GB VRAM is a result; a missing
   706	sudoers line is not. `permission_denied` is deliberately `failed` because it
   707	is always fixable per D-004. `runtime_unavailable` is deliberately
   708	`unsupported` because by D-009 it means "this target composition lacks this
   709	backend", which is an applicability statement; if it occurs because an extra
   710	simply was not installed on a machine that supports it, the run report should
   711	say so and the run be repeated - the controller cannot distinguish these, so
   712	the human/agent in the loop must.
   713	
   714	Consequences: controller implements the table; tests pin it; the known
   715	ambiguity on `runtime_unavailable` is documented in the Phase 2 plan and
   716	flagged in run reports when it occurs.
   717	
   718	Revisit when: a reason code appears that the table misclassifies in practice;
   719	amend the table and this entry rather than special-casing in adapters.
   720	
   721	**AMENDED (magistrate, 2026-08-30, per this entry's own revisit clause):** the
   722	vocabulary is now ten members. `cleanup_failed` (added after this entry was
   723	written) and `model_identity_mismatch` (added by the `_v5` model-identity pin
   724	enforcement, PR #241: the local model's tokenizer/template bytes fail the
   725	pack-carried pin) both map to `failed` — an identity mismatch is an
   726	environment/configuration problem to fix before the run counts, not an
   727	applicability finding. The dividing principle is unchanged. The exact
   728	ten-member table is pinned by the controller tests and the schemas
   729	exact-agreement regression.
   730	
   731	---
   732	
   733	## D-013: Controller-as-DUT mitigation for Mac-local runs
   734	
   735	- Date: 2026-06-09
   736	- Status: accepted
   737	- Phase: 2
   738	
   739	Context: in the Mac vertical slice the controller process runs on the same
   740	machine that `powermetrics` measures. Controller activity during the measured
   741	window pollutes the power trace; this is a measurement-validity threat no
   742	checklist previously named.
   743	
   744	Options considered:
   745	
   746	1. Ignore it. Con: silently biases the flagship measurements.
   747	2. Run the controller from a second machine over SSH even for the Mac. Pro:
   748	   clean separation. Con: requires the SSH transport before the first real
   749	   vertical slice, inverting the planned order; the Mac is the only always
   750	   available device.
   751	3. Co-residency protocol: (a) idle baseline is measured with the controller
   752	   resident and quiescent, so the controller's floor load is inside the
   753	   baseline that gets subtracted; (b) during the measured window the
   754	   controller does nothing but a blocking wait on the runtime (no logging, no
   755	   polling - log records are buffered in memory and flushed after
   756	   `stop_sampling`); (c) the runtime adapter for local runs executes in the
   757	   same process by design, so its cost *is* the workload; (d) document
   758	   residual risk: controller wake-ups are zero by construction, and any OS
   759	   background activity affects idle and load windows alike.
   760	   AMENDED 2026-07-08 (PR #21, `255a7e6`): the quiescent window is
   761	   MARKER-bounded, not call-bounded — the `sampling_stopped` timestamp is
   762	   stamped immediately after the runtime returns, BEFORE adapter alignment
   763	   capture and `stop_sampling` wind-down, so that controller/adapter
   764	   bookkeeping is outside the reducer's measured window. Item (b)'s
   765	   "until stop_sampling" phrasing predates this and reads call-bounded;
   766	   the stamp is the boundary.
   767	
   768	Decision: option 3 for Phase 2, with option 2 recorded as the upgrade path
   769	once the SSH transport exists (re-run a subset and compare).
   770	
   771	Considerations: idle subtraction already exists in the methodology; making
   772	the controller part of the measured system's *idle* state is the cheapest way
   773	to cancel its first-order effect. Deferred logging is essential - a single
   774	log line during a 1 Hz window is a visible artifact.
   775	
   776	Consequences: controller gains an explicit quiescent-wait mode and deferred
   777	log flush; methodology doc gains a Controller Co-Residency section; the
   778	comparison run is queued as a Phase 3-era validation task.
   779	
   780	Revisit when: the SSH-based comparison shows a measurable delta, in which
   781	case Mac headline numbers move to remote-controlled runs.
   782	
   783	---
