   785	## D-014: Statistical protocol for repeated runs
   786	
   787	**2026-08-07 supersession note:** The clauses later superseded by D-053 and
   788	D-077 are retained unchanged as historical context. Current rule ownership:
   789	D-053 and D-077.
   790	
   791	- Date: 2026-06-09
   792	- Status: accepted (draft to be ratified against real variance data at Phase 4
   793	  Stage 4.0)
   794	- Phase: 2 (collection), 4 (analysis)
   795	
   796	Context: acceptance criteria say "repeated runs report variance" and
   797	"uncertainty intervals" with no method defined. Choosing after seeing data
   798	invites motivated choices; choosing now and ratifying with documented
   799	reasoning is auditable.
   800	
   801	Options considered (per element):
   802	
   803	- Repetitions: n=3 (cheap, wide CIs) vs n=5 (headline-defensible, ~2.8x t
   804	  multiplier instead of ~4.3x at 95%) vs n>=10 (hardware time we may not
   805	  have). Chosen: n>=5 for headline comparisons, n>=3 minimum elsewhere,
   806	  recorded per experiment.
   807	- Interval: normal z (wrong at small n) vs Student t (standard at small n,
   808	  assumes rough normality) vs bootstrap percentile (assumption-light but
   809	  unstable at n=5). Chosen: report mean, sample stddev, and 95% t-interval;
   810	  run a bootstrap sensitivity check in Phase 4 and report both where they
   811	  disagree materially.
   812	- Outliers: silent removal (never), keep-all (can bury a real artifact), flag
   813	  via modified z-score on MAD > 3.5 and *report with and without, with the
   814	  physical cause investigated and documented*. Chosen: the latter; a flagged
   815	  point with no identified cause is kept in headline numbers.
   816	- Ordering: condition blocks (thermal drift confounds with condition) vs
   817	  round-robin interleaving (decorrelates slow drift; more model reloads).
   818	  Chosen: round-robin across conditions where reload cost permits, with the
   819	  executed order recorded in the experiment manifest; where blocks are
   820	  operationally forced, that is recorded too.

