   844	## D-015: Split-mechanism priority and same-runtime rule
   845	
   846	- Date: 2026-06-09
   847	- Status: accepted
   848	- Phase: 3
   849	
   850	Context: the headline experiment splits prefill and decode across machines,
   851	which requires moving KV-cache state. KV tensors are runtime-specific
   852	(layout, dtype, quantization, RoPE handling), and not every runtime can
   853	export or import them. This is the project's largest feasibility risk
   854	(R-004, R-005).
   855	
   856	Options considered:
   857	
   858	1. Live KV streaming between runtimes first (Splitwise-style). Pro: closest
   859	   to the inspiration paper. Con: hardest variant of the riskiest component;
   860	   no public stable path in vLLM, none at all across heterogeneous runtimes;
   861	   a failure here late in the schedule sinks the phase.
   862	2. Cross-runtime transfer via a translation layer (e.g., vLLM prefill ->
   863	   MLX decode). Con: deep model-internals work, research-grade in itself,
   864	   out of scope for a measurement capstone.
   865	3. Feasibility-ordered ladder with a guaranteed floor:
   866	   a. *Synthetic transfer microbenchmark* (always feasible): move
   867	      KV-sized payloads between nodes with both-end power sampling. Yields
   868	      transfer energy/time vs payload size vs link speed regardless of any
   869	      runtime's cooperation.
   870	   b. *Offline replay* (primary mechanism): same runtime family on both
   871	      ends; prefill on node A, persist the prompt cache to a file, transfer
   872	      it, resume decode from it on node B. Candidate paths: mlx-lm prompt
   873	      cache save/load; llama.cpp `--prompt-cache` session files; vLLM
   874	      expected unsupported for file replay (spike confirms).
   875	   c. *Live split* (stretch): socket streaming during a run, only attempted
   876	      after (b) produces publishable data.
   877	   Rule: same runtime (and pinned version) on both ends of any real KV
   878	   transfer; cross-runtime KV portability is explicitly out of scope, and
   879	   heterogeneous *hardware* pairs are achieved with a portable runtime
   880	   (llama.cpp) where its backends allow.
   881	
   882	Decision: option 3.
   883	
   884	Considerations: the ladder converts an existential risk into a bounded one:
   885	even if every runtime spike fails, (a) plus Phase 2 homogeneous baselines
   886	still supports an analytical split-energy model (prefill energy measured on
   887	A, decode energy measured on B via replay-or-monolithic decomposition,
   888	transfer energy measured synthetically), which is an honest, defensible
   889	capstone result. Each rung up improves directness of measurement. The
   890	same-runtime rule eliminates the one problem (tensor portability across
   891	engines) that no amount of harness engineering can fix on schedule.
   892	Open question carried into the spikes: llama.cpp session-file portability
   893	across *backends/platforms* (CUDA-save -> Metal-load) is unverified and gets
   894	its own spike with an explicit verdict.
   895	
   896	Consequences: Phase 3 plan is structured around the ladder (Stage 3.0
   897	spikes before any hardware scheduling); the KV-size analytical model feeds
   898	payload sizes for (a); verdict codes per runtime are recorded in
   899	`docs/phase_3/kv_feasibility.md`.
   900	
   901	Revisit when: a spike contradicts an assumption (then the ladder re-ranks,
   902	documented), or vLLM's disaggregation API stabilizes early enough to matter.
   903	
