  1013	## D-018: Per-backend `power_w` definition and rail policy
  1014	
  1015	- Date: 2026-06-09
  1016	- Status: accepted
  1017	- Phase: 2+
  1018	
  1019	Context: `power_trace.csv` has `power_w`, `source`, and optional `rail`
  1020	columns, but "power" means different physical boundaries on different
  1021	backends (SoC subsystems vs GPU board vs module input vs wall AC). Without a
  1022	fixed definition, cross-target comparisons silently compare different
  1023	quantities.
  1024	
  1025	Options considered:
  1026	
  1027	1. One `power_w` row per sample, backend decides what it means. Con: loses
  1028	   per-rail information; the meaning varies invisibly.
  1029	2. Per-rail rows only, reducers sum everything. Con: "everything" differs by
  1030	   backend; accidental double counting (e.g., a backend reporting both
  1031	   package and per-subsystem rails).
  1032	3. Per-rail rows preserved as reported, plus a per-backend *rail manifest*
  1033	   that names exactly which rails sum to the backend's canonical `power_w`
  1034	   for reduction, and a methodology table stating each backend's physical
  1035	   measurement boundary. powermetrics: cpu_power + gpu_power + ane_power
  1036	   (SoC subsystem proxy; excludes display, storage, PSU losses). nvidia-smi:
  1037	   board power as reported (GPU board only; excludes host). jetson_rails:
  1038	   VDD_IN preferred (module input) with the actually-used rail recorded.
  1039	   wall_meter: AC wall power (full system).
  1040	
  1041	Decision: option 3.
  1042	
  1043	Considerations: per-rail rows keep raw fidelity (Apple's per-subsystem split
  1044	is itself interesting data); the manifest makes the summation auditable and
  1045	fixable post hoc; the boundary table converts an implicit comparability
  1046	problem into an explicit, reportable limitation - cross-target absolute
  1047	comparisons must state boundaries, and wall-meter deltas (when the meter
  1048	exists, P1-003) calibrate the gap.
  1049	
  1050	Consequences: telemetry adapters declare their rail manifest in
  1051	`device_metadata`; reducer sums per the manifest; methodology gains the
  1052	Measurement Boundaries section; the limitations section of the final report
  1053	inherits the boundary table.
  1054	
  1055	Revisit when: a backend exposes a strictly better boundary (e.g., macOS adds
  1056	package-level wall-equivalent reporting).
  1057	
