  1060	## D-019: Mock adapters use simulated time via an injectable clock
  1061	
  1062	- Date: 2026-06-09
  1063	- Status: accepted
  1064	- Phase: 2
  1065	
  1066	Context: reducer correctness tests need traces and events whose expected
  1067	energy is computable in closed form; the controller needs real time for real
  1068	runs. If mocks sleep through real seconds, tests get slow and flaky; if the
  1069	controller special-cases mocks, the lifecycle under test diverges from the
  1070	real one.
  1071	
  1072	Options considered:
  1073	
  1074	1. Mocks sleep in real time. Con: a 30 s idle window makes the test suite
  1075	   unusable; timestamp jitter breaks exact assertions.
  1076	2. Controller branches on mock backends. Con: the code path being tested is
  1077	   no longer the production path.
  1078	3. A minimal `Clock` protocol (`now() -> float`, `sleep(seconds)`) injected
  1079	   into the controller; `SystemClock` for real runs, `FakeClock` that
  1080	   advances instantly for tests and mock runs. Mock adapters compute
  1081	   deterministic timestamps/samples from the config and the injected clock;
  1082	   the controller code path is identical in both modes.
  1083	
  1084	Decision: option 3.
  1085	
  1086	Considerations: this is the standard seam for time-dependent systems; it
  1087	keeps the mock vertical slice fast (CI, D-017), exact (reducer tests assert
  1088	energy to the float), and honest (same controller code). The mock telemetry
  1089	trace is specified in Slice 2B as piecewise-constant power levels per
  1090	lifecycle stage so trapezoidal integration has a closed-form expectation.
  1091	
  1092	Consequences: controller and adapters take a clock parameter; no module ever
  1093	calls `time.time()`/`time.sleep()` directly except `SystemClock`.
  1094	
  1095	Revisit when: never expected.
  1096	
  1097	---
