183:| D-156 | SUPERSESSION WRITE-TIME REFUSAL (SUPERSESSION-DUP-REFUSAL-01 ruling, magistrate 2026-08-27 over a Sol xhigh read-only seat + Opus lieutenant packet, both agreeing on every axis; packet `docs/process_traces/2026-08-27-t26/supersession-dup-ruling-packet.md`): the supersession recorder REFUSES, before row construction, any write whose `bundle_id` already has a recognizable row in the target campaign log — unconditionally, keyed on `bundle_id` within one log, with NO requirement that the existing row validate (an invalid competing disposition never licenses a second write); registered reason code `campaign_occurrence_supersession_already_recorded` on the existing `LaunchLineageError` pattern; no consumer change; no byte repair owed. Facts the ruling rests on: same-bytes idempotency is not an available option (`utc_timestamp()` is inside `entry_sha256`); the immediate double-run is already fail-closed in both consumers (cooldown join and whole-window membership each demand exactly one row); a later THIRD occurrence produces a cross-consumer divergence (membership selects, cooldown refuses, identical bytes); D-093's supersession audit is blind to it (compares totals only); zero supersession rows exist in any reachable campaign log. Residuals: Q1 later failure of an already-selected occurrence → A (fresh root / non-claim disposition) now, chained-supersession schema minted as a QUEUED row (latest-wins rejected on ruled grounds — `whole_window_verdict_conflict`); Q2 historical duplicates → A (preserve and refuse; retraction design separately; rewrite rejected); Q3 off-machine Window B/C per-`bundle_id` scan → A MANDATORY before any consumption of those corpora, documented in runbook §11; Q4 the cross-consumer divergence → B its own queued row (a fail-closed consumer change does not ride a write-time-guard PR); Q5 this entry is the ruling the row's acceptance requires. | adopted (magistrate; seat + lieutenant unanimous) |
