TASK_QUEUE.md:112:| MLX-ACID-SIGABRT-01 | P2 Next Slice | 2026-08-27 | Cure the pre-existing pytest SIGABRT at `joulewise/adapters/mlx_runtime.py:1159` reached via the four ACID tests in `tests/test_arm_readiness_evidence_t0.py`, so the suite is pytest-collectable without deselection | No phase exit-checklist matrix row exists. Landed on PR #203 (branch `fix/ci-reliability-batch`; three rounds: the cure, the audit fix round, and the delta re-audit round that is the branch head). The abort is a nanobind double-registration: `mlx.core` is a C-extension whose native initializer calls `abort()` if it runs twice, and the hidden diagnostic is `Critical nanobind error: refusing to add duplicate key "cpu" to enumeration "mlx.core.DeviceType"!`. It ran twice because `author_environment` injected its synthetic `mlx_lm` with `mock.patch.dict(sys.modules, ...)`, whose exit does `clear(); update(<snapshot taken on entry>)` — `mlx`/`mlx.core` are imported LAZILY inside that context by `_mlx_metal_memory`, so they were absent from the snapshot, were evicted on exit, and the next test's `import_module` re-executed the loaded shared object. Cured code-side on both halves per the row's fence (a process-level abort cannot be contained by a marker): `_resolve_mlx_core()` keeps a reference to the loaded extension, and `author_environment` patches the single `mlx_lm` key. The resolver took THREE audit rounds to get right, and the final rule is the record: `sys.modules` is authoritative while it holds an entry; a `None` entry is Python's blocked-import sentinel and still raises `mlx_core_not_found`; membership in the cache turns on what a module IS (`ExtensionFileLoader`) and not on who imported it — the first shape cached whatever sat in `sys.modules` (test stand-ins would have outlived their own `finally` blocks and fed real evidence), the second cached only self-imports (missing the real extension that `mlx_lm.load()` imports before the snapshot, leaving the abort reachable by another route); and the resolver RESTORES the evicted key rather than stepping around it, because while the key is missing any importer in the process — not just this adapter — re-runs the initializer. Seven defect-shaped regressions, each red-before/green-after: `test_mlx_metal_memory_reuses_cached_core_after_module_eviction`, `test_author_environment_preserves_modules_imported_inside_context`, and the five `MlxCoreResolutionTests` (`..._standin_never_enters_the_extension_cache`, `..._blocked_import_sentinel_is_honoured_over_the_cache`, `..._extension_imported_by_another_component_is_remembered`, `..._resolving_after_an_eviction_repairs_sys_modules`, `..._a_standin_left_in_the_cache_is_discarded_not_served`). Acceptance: pre-fix `pytest -k acid` exit 134 with `Fatal Python error: Aborted`; post-fix, same command with the skips temporarily removed, exit 1 `3 failed, 1 skipped, 32 deselected` and a normal summary; the four ACID skips now read `STRUCTURAL-BLOCKED: fixture R1 schemas require FIXTURE-MODERNIZATION-01 (A84)` (the canonical census prefix — the first attempt wrote `STRUCTURE-BLOCKED:` and broke `tests/test_s0_blocked_enumeration.py` with `AssertionError: 0 != 4`, caught by audit and independently by CI on the pre-fix head; the census moves 17/4 to 21/0) and stay skipped, because this row was NEVER sufficient for them — A84 is; `pytest --collect-only -q` reports 4008 tests collected with zero deselection. NOT done here: A84 itself. RESIDUAL RISK recorded, not fixed: the reference lives in the adapter module global, so an `importlib.reload` of the adapter would reset it; no reloader exists anywhere in `joulewise/`, `scripts/` or `tests/`, and the key-restoration above makes the state self-repairing on the next probe. Custody: `docs/process_traces/2026-08-26-t26-ci-reliability/`. |
TASK_QUEUE.md:113:| CALEXITS-FOURTH-SHAPE-01 | P2 Next Slice | 2026-08-27 | Complete the mutation-race terminal-shape taxonomy after run 32739939880 failed `test_forced_auto_maintenance_mutation_reproduces_cleanup_race` with a fourth shape the widened predicate rejects | No phase exit-checklist matrix row exists. Closed on verification via PR #203; the CURE had already landed at `ddb1f633` on 2026-08-24, after this row's text was authored, and the row simply predated it. The preserved attempt-1 log (custodied at `docs/process_traces/2026-08-26-t26-ci-reliability/evidence/calexits-fourth-shape-run32739939880-attempt1-py3.11.txt`, from an archive with sha256 `f60638ebcea81bf5584a722604760a6e617ff6df70c1251222e0e2c486d8108c`) shows `RACE_EXERCISED=0 NO_RACE_PRE_WRITE=0 TRACE_INCOMPLETE=0` — every counter zero, because the test died on its first attempt at `self.assertTrue(pack_evidence.complete, ...)` before classifying anything — and an event tail in which the detached `git maintenance` process enters `loose-objects`, spawns only `git prune-packed --quiet` (exit 0) and exits 0. No `git pack-objects` child was ever spawned: the absent-pack-child shape. The 3.14 job of the same run passed with `RACE_EXERCISED=1`, which is why the shape read as intermittent rather than as a broken predicate. `ddb1f633` covers exactly that — `_classify_pack_cleanup` branches on `evidence.pack_child_absent` FIRST and returns `NO_PACK_CHILD`, the mutation test guards its completeness assertion with `if not pack_evidence.pack_child_absent:`, and a raised cleanup errno still outranks the absent child so it cannot mask a real shape-A race. Each of the five modelled shapes A–E carries a deterministic `_synthetic_pack_topology` regression, and that builder raises `"synthetic topology terminal shapes are mutually exclusive"` if asked for two at once. Stated precisely, because an audit round caught the overclaim: that proves MUTUAL EXCLUSION among the modelled shapes, NOT that the taxonomy is exhaustive — a sixth topology would classify `TRACE_INCOMPLETE` and fail loudly rather than pass silently, which is the property soundness needs. Acceptance evidence: bench, the full `tests/test_calibration_exits.py` module passes `46 passed, 208 subtests passed`; hosted, of the 36 `main` runs whose head descends from both this cure and `a28b55bf`, 31 are green, and none of the 5 failures is this test (four are `test_gen_state` live-ID oracle drift during 2026-08-26/27 bookkeeping; the fifth, run 32813091203, is the identity-pins teardown race cured on the same PR). |
TASK_QUEUE.md:114:| PLANTEST-RGLOB-RACE-01 | P2 Next Slice | 2026-08-27 | Cure the `checkout_inventory` rglob/scandir race in `tests/test_d117_decode_contrast_plan.py` (run 32745254371: `os.scandir` died mid-walk when a directory vanished during the recursive inventory) | No phase exit-checklist matrix row exists. Closed on verification via PR #203; the CURE had already landed at `a28b55bf` on 2026-08-24, after this row's text was authored. `checkout_inventory` no longer uses `Path.rglob`: it walks with `os.walk(topdown=True, onerror=<re-raise>)` and prunes `.git` and `__pycache__` from `dirnames` before descending, so the git loose-object fan-out directory that vanished under a background repack is never scandir-ed at all. Exactness is deliberately preserved rather than traded for tolerance — a real, non-excluded directory vanishing mid-walk still raises, and the regression says so in as many words. Three deterministic regressions ship with it, driven by `_VanishingDirectoryHarness`, which patches `os.scandir` to pause the walking thread at a chosen directory while a worker thread `rmtree`s it: `test_git_object_store_is_never_walked_and_cannot_toll_the_inventory` (cites the hosted provenance verbatim and asserts the hook never even fires — not firing IS the green result), `test_inventory_raises_when_a_real_directory_vanishes_mid_walk`, and `test_inventory_matches_the_rglob_semantics_it_replaces` (symlinks, dangling links, empty directories). Acceptance evidence: bench, `tests/test_d117_decode_contrast_plan.py` three consecutive runs at `24 passed, 1 skipped, 24 subtests passed` each; hosted, the same 36-run/31-green census recorded on CALEXITS-FOURTH-SHAPE-01 above, in which this race did not recur. Same failure family as the identity-pins teardown race cured on PR #203 and as `EVIDENCE-AUTHOR-GIT-TEARDOWN-01`: a background git process mutating a fixture tree another thread is walking or removing. |
