     1	# The "ruled but never installed" sweep (S9, T26, 2026-08-27)
     2	
     3	## What this is
     4	
     5	A read-only audit of every decision the project made between **D-117 and
     6	D-157**, asking one question of each: *the thing that was ruled — is it actually
     7	in the code?*
     8	
     9	Not "was it written down." Not "did someone say it landed." Is there, today, at
    10	a line number you can open, an artifact that does the ruled thing — and a check
    11	that refuses the wrong value at the place where the bytes are made.
    12	
    13	## Why it was run
    14	
    15	Three times in the single session of 2026-08-26/27, a ruled contract turned out
    16	to have **no route or no check in code**:
    17	
    18	1. **CONSUME-CONFIRMATION-SUPPLY-01** — the launcher had no supply line for the
    19	   step-6 confirmation table the ceremony required.
    20	2. **The launcher runbook arguments** — the code required arguments no runbook
    21	   passed.
    22	3. **D-157** — on 2026-08-17, ruling D-139 clause A2 fixed a Holm multiplicity
    23	   family at m=2 and said those values "enter the gamma prospective manifest's
    24	   `families` block at the production freeze." Ten days and eighteen decisions
    25	   later the generator still emitted `m=1` with a "contingent on unresolved
    26	   ratification" note, no `families` block at all, and the freeze path never ran
    27	   the prospective validator. The clone proof passed end to end **while minting
    28	   bytes the consumption edge would refuse.** Cost, had it not been caught by a
    29	   reader: a 168-hour campaign ending in a dead claim edge.
    30	
    31	Each of the three was found by a person reading, not by the instrument. That is
    32	the defect this sweep exists to size: **how many more of these are there.**
    33	
    34	## The answer, in one line
    35	
    36	Enough that the transaction night should not be scheduled on the assumption that
    37	"ruled" means "installed." The sweep found a second end-to-end instance of the
    38	D-157 shape — this one on the collector, downstream of D-157 and **not cured by
    39	it** — plus a scope correction that the in-flight W-10 cure needs before it can
    40	succeed.
    41	
    42	## How to read the files here
    43	
    44	| File | What it holds |
    45	| --- | --- |
    46	| `SHORTLIST.md` | **Read this first.** The transaction-blocking findings, ranked, each with the concrete cure and whether that cure is a code change, a runbook change, or a W-list entry. |
    47	| `FINDINGS-TABLE.md` | Every implementation clause the sweep enumerated, with its status and evidence. The full census. |
    48	| `METHOD.md` | What was swept, against what baseline, what the four statuses mean, and how the seats were run and refuted. Read this before disputing a status. |
    49	| `raw/` | Verbatim per-group seat output and the refuter reports, preserved as custody. `raw/SHARED-BRIEF.md` is the binding brief every enumeration seat ran under. |
    50	
    51	## The status codes in brief
    52	
    53	**A** — installed and checked at the producer. **B** — the rule exists somewhere
    54	but nothing enforces it where the bytes are produced; a consumer refuses instead.
    55	**C** — not installed at all. **D** — superseded by a later ruling.
    56	
    57	**B is the D-157 shape**, and it is the category that costs campaign nights,
    58	because a B failure is invisible until after the spend. The single test that
    59	separates B from A: *does the validator have any caller outside its own module
    60	and its tests?* `validate_prospective_analysis_manifest_v3` did not — it was
    61	correct, complete, and called by nothing on the freeze path, for ten days.
    62	
    63	## What this sweep feeds
    64	
    65	The unratified process proposal at
    66	`../process-proposals/ruling-status-semantics.md` argues that a ruling with an
    67	implementation clause should be recorded as `decided`, not `done`, until its
    68	index row cites the commit that installed it **and** names the producer-side
    69	check that refuses its absence. That proposal routes to a cold gate, and this
    70	sweep is the evidence packet for it. The census below is what "we do not
    71	currently know which rulings are installed" costs, measured.
    72	
    73	## Standing caveat
    74	
    75	This audit can miss things — a clause whose installation lives somewhere no seat
    76	looked. It is built not to invent things: every finding on the shortlist survived
    77	an independent seat whose instructions were to prove it installed. Read the
    78	counts as a floor, not a census.
     1	# S9 sweep — method of record
     2	
     3	## What was swept, and against what baseline
     4	
     5	**Corpus.** Every decision in `docs/decision_log.md` from **D-117 through
     6	D-157** — both the one-line index rows (lines 142-184) and the tail sections
     7	that carry each decision's full text — plus the twenty-six **unattributed tail
     8	sections** of the same era. Those unattributed sections carry a `WO-…` or
     9	`R-N RULED` heading instead of a D-number (for example "WO-CONSUMPTION-EDGE
    10	contract ADOPTED", `:9112-9159`), but they are rulings with implementation
    11	clauses exactly like the numbered ones, and excluding them would have skipped
    12	the consumption-edge and launch-binding contracts — two of the three areas the
    13	sweep was called to examine.
    14	
    15	**Baseline.** `origin/main` at `0dd3b6dc` ("MLX-ACID-SIGABRT-01 +
    16	CALEXITS-FOURTH-SHAPE-01 + PLANTEST-RGLOB-RACE-01 closed", merged 2026-08-26).
    17	Every `file:line` citation in this trace was read at that commit. Work in flight
    18	on other branches — notably the S8 stream on `fix/d139-a2-gamma-families`, which
    19	implements D-157's W-10 cure — is NOT baseline; where a finding is already cured
    20	on an unmerged branch, the table says so.
    21	
    22	## What counts as an implementation clause
    23	
    24	An **implementation clause** is any sentence in a ruling that asserts a value,
    25	rule, check, refusal, emission, schema key, threshold, or documentation change
    26	is (or will be) realized in an artifact — code, a config, generated pack bytes,
    27	a schema, a runbook, a script, or a test. The signal verbs the sweep keyed on:
    28	*enters, is installed, the code refuses, lands as, the generator emits, the
    29	runbook says, at the production freeze, must, shall, gains, is added, is
    30	stamped, regressions:, the validator requires.*
    31	
    32	Pure authority rulings were excluded — "delegated to the magistrate", "Ed signed
    33	off", "recorded as D-1xx" — unless they also specified a mechanical check or a
    34	required document.
    35	
    36	## The four statuses
    37	
    38	| Code | Meaning |
    39	| --- | --- |
    40	| **A** | Installed AND checked at the producer. The artifact that *writes* the bytes enforces the ruled thing, and a check refuses the wrong value at write time. |
    41	| **B** | Installed, but with NO producer-side check. The value or rule exists somewhere — a consumer refuses it, a validator function exists, a doc says it — but nothing enforces it where the bytes are produced. **This is the D-157 shape.** |
    42	| **C** | Not installed. No code, config, pack byte, runbook, or test realizes the clause. |
    43	| **D** | Superseded by a later ruling (cited). |
    44	
    45	The decisive rule, applied throughout: **a validator function with no callers
    46	outside its own module and tests is a B, not an A.** That single test is what
    47	separates "the rule exists" from "the rule is enforced," and it is the test that
    48	D-157 failed for ten days — `validate_prospective_analysis_manifest_v3` existed,
    49	was correct, and was called by nothing on the freeze path.
    50	
    51	Seats were instructed to prefer B over A whenever torn, on the ground that a
    52	false A is the worst outcome this sweep can produce: it is exactly the error
    53	that let D-157 live.
    54	
    55	## How the sweep was run
    56	
    57	**Enumeration — ten parallel seats, one group each.** Seven Opus 5 agents took
    58	the numbered decisions (D-117..D-121, D-122..D-125, D-126..D-132, D-133..D-134,
    59	D-135..D-141, D-142..D-149, D-150..D-157); three Sol xhigh seats took the
    60	unattributed tail sections. Every seat ran under the same binding brief
    61	(`raw/SHARED-BRIEF.md`), read its assigned line ranges verbatim before extracting
    62	anything, and was required to produce a `file:line` it had personally opened for
    63	every status assigned. Raw per-group output is preserved under `raw/`.
    64	
    65	**Refutation — independent seats, distinct lenses.** Per the C-028 gauntlet,
    66	each transaction-relevant B and C finding was handed to seats whose instructions
    67	were to PROVE IT INSTALLED, not to confirm it. Blocker-severity findings got two
    68	refuters with distinct lenses — **contract** (what was ruled, what the governing
    69	document says, what a later ruling superseded) and **execution** (what actually
    70	runs, in order, on the night). A finding appears in the confirmed table only if
    71	its refuters failed to break it; refuted and partially-refuted findings are
    72	recorded with the refutation, because a finding that dies to a good refutation is
    73	still evidence about where the instrument is weak.
    74	
    75	## Standing bias of this method
    76	
    77	The sweep can produce false negatives — a clause whose installation lives
    78	somewhere no seat looked. It is designed not to produce false positives: every
    79	confirmed finding survived an adversary with the opposite brief. Read the counts
    80	as a floor, not a census.
